+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdMK2Jwt4bgsbDwLxZF7Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Built and verified — `npm run build`, `npm run lint`, and `npm test` (26 tests) all pass, and the server was smoke-tested end to end over HTTP.\n\n## What's there\n\n**`src/permissions.ts`** — the core of the maze. `effectiveRole()` combines three sources and takes the max under `viewer < editor < owner`:\n- creator ownership,\n- direct shares on the resource,\n- shares inherited from *every* ancestor folder (walked with a cycle guard).\n\nSo a weak direct share never shrinks a stronger inherited one, and a share on a root folder reaches documents nested arbitrarily deep.\n\n**`src/store.ts`** — in-memory `Map`s, scrypt-salted passwords, random bearer tokens, activity log. One `Store` per `createApp()`, so state resets on restart and each test gets a clean world.\n\n**`src/routes/`** — auth, folders, documents, share, activity. Route aliases are registered where the spec listed alternatives (`/auth/register` + `/users`, `/documents` + `/folders/:id/documents`, per-resource `/share` + generic `/share`), and every route is also mounted under `/api`.\n\n**Permission rules enforced:** viewers read only; editors read/modify/share but cannot grant above their own role or delete; owners do everything including revoking shares; the creator's ownership can't be reassigned; folder deletion cascades over the subtree.\n\n**Status codes:** 401 missing/invalid token, 403 authenticated-but-under-privileged, 404 unknown resource, 400 bad payload, 409 duplicate username, 204 deletes.\n\n## Two judgment calls worth flagging\n\n- **403 vs 404 for inaccessible resources.** I return 403 when a resource exists but you lack access, rather than masking it as 404. That's the standard reading of the spec's \"appropriate status codes\", but it does confirm existence to an unauthorized caller. Say the word if you'd prefer 404-masking.